@startuml FRONTEND_ARCHITECTURE_DIAGRAM
' ==============================================================================
' HOW TO RENDER THIS FILE
' ==============================================================================
'   1. VS Code: install the "PlantUML" extension (by jebbs), open this file,
'      press Alt+D for a live preview (or right-click -> "Preview Current
'      Diagram"). This is the easiest option while you're learning the code.
'   2. Web: paste this whole file into https://www.plantuml.com/plantuml/uml/
'   3. CLI: `plantuml FRONTEND_ARCHITECTURE_DIAGRAM.puml` (needs Java +
'      Graphviz).
'
' The companion file FRONTEND_ARCHITECTURE_DIAGRAM_LAYERED_VIEW.puml shows the
' same code organised by pattern rather than by folder. Read them together: these
' answer "where is this file", that one answers "why is it shaped that way".
' ==============================================================================
' THIS FILE HOLDS TWO DIAGRAMS
' ==============================================================================
' Search for @startuml to jump between them. The folder map was split in two on
' 2026-09-27 because a single picture of every folder had grown about 12,800
' pixels wide and was no longer readable at any zoom.
'
'   DIAGRAM 1  THE APP AROUND THE FORM - entry point, routes, shell, auth,
'              translations, theme, the api layer, and user administration.
'              protokoll/ appears here as one summary box.
'   DIAGRAM 2  INSIDE protokoll/ - the eleven folders that make up the survey
'              form itself, which is where nearly all the code is.
'
' Read 1 first. It is the shape of the application; 2 is the shape of its one
' big feature.
' ==============================================================================
' KEEPING THIS FILE HONEST
' ==============================================================================
' Last checked against frontend/src: 2026-09-27, after feature 16c.
'
' This is a map of real folders and real file names, so it rots quietly: a
' renamed file or a new feature folder leaves a diagram that still looks
' authoritative. Any change that adds, removes or renames a folder under
' frontend/src, or a route in routes.tsx, has to change this file in the same
' branch.
' ==============================================================================
' A NOTE FOR SOMEONE NEW TO FRONTEND DEVELOPMENT
' ==============================================================================
' React's whole job is: take some data, and turn it into what's on screen,
' and turn what the user does back into changed data. Every pattern below
' exists to answer one of two questions:
'   (a) WHERE does a piece of data/state live, and who is allowed to change it?
'   (b) HOW do we avoid writing the same logic (a fetch call, a validation
'       rule, an accessibility attribute) in twenty different components?
' Keep those two questions in mind while reading the diagrams; almost every
' folder below is an answer to one of them.
' ==============================================================================

skinparam componentStyle rectangle
skinparam packageStyle folder
skinparam wrapWidth 260
skinparam maxMessageSize 260

header FRONTEND FOLDER MAP - frontend/src/**
title
  DIAGRAM 1 of 2 - THE APP AROUND THE FORM
  everything except protokoll/, which is Diagram 2 below
  (what lives where, and the one-line job of each folder)
end title

package "main.tsx  (the entry point)" as entry #FFF3CD {
  note as EntryNote
    Runs ONCE, before anything else. Sets up, from the outside in:
    ThemeProvider (MUI + our tokens) -> QueryClientProvider (TanStack
    Query cache, lives ABOVE the router so it survives every
    navigation) -> DatumsProvider (date-formatting locale context) ->
    RouterProvider (routes.tsx). Order matters: whatever wraps
    whatever else is available to it via React Context.
  end note
}

package "routes.tsx  (route table)" as routes #FFF3CD {
  note as RoutesNote
    A plain data structure (createBrowserRouter([...])) mapping URL
    paths to components. Paths are German, decided 2026-08-24, like
    the rest of the domain; component names around them stay English.

    /anmeldung is the one PUBLIC route, and the one outside the app
    shell: a header with an account area, on the page where nobody has
    an account, reads as a mistake. Every other route is nested inside
    <SitzungsWaechter>, so "you must be signed in" is written ONCE
    here instead of once per page. This nesting is the "layout route"
    pattern: a parent route renders shared chrome (or, for the guard,
    a redirect-or-nothing decision) and an <Outlet/> where the matched
    child route appears.

    The routes, as of feature 16c:
    |= Path |= Screen |
    | ""/"" | own protocols, or the review queue |
    | ""/protokolle/neu"" | redirects to section 1 of "neu" |
    | ""/protokolle/:id/abschnitt/:nr"" | the form, one section at a time |
    | ""/protokolle/:id/pruefung"" | a handed-in protocol, read only |
    | ""/pruefung"" | the review queue, filters in the URL |
    | ""/verwaltung/benutzer"" | every account, Super Admin only |
    | ""/verwaltung/benutzer/neu"" | creating one |

    **No route carries a role requirement**, deliberately. The server
    decides who may read what, in one place, and a second opinion here
    could only ever be the wrong one. Hiding a route is not a
    permission; the pages turn the server's refusal into words with a
    way onward (KeineBerechtigung.tsx).
  end note
}

package "components/  (app shell, shared chrome)" as shellpkg #E8F4FD {
  [Layout.tsx]
  [AppShell.tsx]
  [SiteHeader.tsx]
  [SiteFooter.tsx]
  [ThemeToggle.tsx]
  [NotFound.tsx]
  [BestaetigungsDialog.tsx]
  [DateiPicker.tsx]
  [icons.tsx]
  note bottom of shellpkg
    Layout.tsx wraps every page in AppShell (header/footer/skip-link)
    and renders <Outlet/> for whichever page matched. This is why
    navigating between sections never re-mounts the header: only the
    <Outlet/>'s content changes.

    BestaetigungsDialog.tsx and DateiPicker.tsx are here rather than
    in a feature folder because more than one feature needs them: the
    dialog is asked for before deleting a draft, discarding one and
    removing an attachment; the picker is used by both attachment
    blocks and by the PDF import.
  end note
}

package "hooks/  (app-wide hooks)" as hookspkg #E8F4FD {
  [useTheme.ts]
}

package "auth/  (who is signed in)" as authpkg #E8F4FD {
  [AnmeldungSeite.tsx] as AnmeldungSeite
  [SitzungsWaechter.tsx] as Waechter
  [AbmeldeKnopf.tsx]
  [useSitzung.ts] as useSitzung
  [rollen.ts]
  [weiter.ts]
  [startseite.ts]
  [useKontoSprache.ts]
  [anmeldung.css]
  note bottom of authpkg
    In one line: useSitzung.ts owns a React Query cache entry that
    mirrors the backend's answer to GET /api/v1/ich; SitzungsWaechter.tsx
    is the guard that reads it and either redirects to /anmeldung or
    renders <Outlet/>. DATA_FLOW_DIAGRAM.puml traces that call end to
    end (its section 0, and steps 13-18 for the backend half).

    weiter.ts and startseite.ts are the two pure decisions around it:
    where to send somebody back to after signing in (validated, so a
    ?weiter= parameter cannot be turned into an open redirect), and
    which screen is "home" for the roles they hold. rollen.ts is the
    one order the six roles are printed in, lifted out of SiteHeader
    in feature 16b when the account list became the second place that
    prints them.
  end note
}

package "i18n/  (translations)" as i18npkg #E8F4FD {
  [index.ts]
  [resources.d.ts]
  [sprachen.ts]
  [DatumsProvider.tsx]
  note bottom of i18npkg
    react-i18next. No component ever hard-codes a user-facing string;
    every one is a t('some.key') lookup into a German (later English)
    resource file. resources.d.ts makes the KEYS themselves type-checked,
    so t('anmeldung.titeel') (typo) is a compile error, not a blank label.
    DatumsProvider.tsx carries the matching date/number locale, so a
    date is never formatted by one language while its label reads in
    another.
  end note
}

package "theme/  +  styles/  (design tokens -> MUI)" as themepkg #E8F4FD {
  [tokens.ts]
  [muiTheme.ts]
  [styles/theme.css]
  note bottom of themepkg
    styles/theme.css defines raw CSS custom properties (colour, radius,
    spacing) for light AND dark mode. muiTheme.ts reads those tokens
    ONCE and configures MUI's theme from them, and per-component
    styling overrides live there too (coding-standards.md: "theme a
    component once, not per use"). No component should ever hard-code
    a colour.
  end note
}

package "api/  (the ONLY place fetch() is called)" as apipkg #D4EDDA {
  [client.ts (apiAnfrage, apiDatei)] as client
  [fehler.ts (ApiFehler + codes)]
  [useFehlertext.ts]
  [wiederholen.ts (sollWiederholen)]
  [queryClient.ts]
  [typen.ts]
  note bottom of apipkg
    **client.ts is a tiny hand-built "HTTP service layer".** Every
    feature-specific api.ts (see entwurf/api.ts below) calls
    apiAnfrage() here instead of calling fetch() itself. This is the
    Repository/Service pattern: components and hooks never know HTTP
    exists, they just call a named function that returns typed data
    or throws a typed ApiFehler.

    apiDatei() is the second entry point, added in feature 23e: one
    call that answers with a FILE rather than JSON. Separate from
    apiAnfrage rather than a flag on it, because the two differ in
    what a success IS - that one parses a body, this one must not
    touch it. The refusal path is shared, so a refused download says
    what went wrong in German beside the button that was pressed.

    wiederholen.ts is the one retry policy for every list in the app:
    a dropped request is tried again, a settled refusal (not signed
    in, role missing) never is, since making somebody wait through
    three requests for the same no helps nobody.
    queryClient.ts creates the ONE TanStack Query cache/client used
    by the whole app (created in main.tsx's <QueryClientProvider>).
  end note
}


package "protokoll/  (the feature: the survey form itself)" as protopkg #FDEBD0 {
  [liste/, pruefliste/, pruefung/  - the three list and review screens]
  [ProtokollSeite + ProtokollFormular, abschnitte/, felder/]
  [entwurf/ (autosave), regeln/ (validation), absenden/]
  [einlesen/ (PDF in), ausgabe/ (PDF out), anlagen/, nurlesen/]
  note bottom of protopkg
    The survey form and everything around it: eleven folders, drawn
    in full in **DIAGRAM 2** further down this file. It is a feature
    area rather than a layer, so it has its own pages, its own api.ts
    files and its own pure-logic modules, all of which go through
    api/client.ts like anything else.
  end note
}

package "verwaltung/benutzer/  (user administration, feature 16)" as verwaltung #FDEBD0 {
  [BenutzerlisteSeite.tsx]
  [BenutzerTabelle.tsx, BenutzerZeile.tsx]
  [Suchfeld.tsx, suche.ts, anzeige.ts]
  [KontoAnlegenSeite.tsx, useKontoAnlegen.ts]
  [Kontofeld.tsx, Rollenauswahl.tsx, Zugangsdaten.tsx]
  [eingabe.ts, fehlerfelder.ts]
  [api.ts, abfragen.ts]
  [KeineBerechtigung.tsx, LeererZustand.tsx, Ladefehler.tsx]
  note bottom of verwaltung
    The second feature area, and the first thing under /verwaltung.
    There is no sign-up page anywhere in this application: every
    account is created by a Super Admin here (or, for the very first
    one, by the backend's own CLI). The password is typed by the
    administrator setting it; the app never invents one.

    Same shape as protokoll/: a *Seite.tsx container per route, pure
    functions beside them for anything where a wrong answer is
    possible (eingabe.ts validates what was typed, fehlerfelder.ts
    maps a server refusal back onto the field that caused it), and one
    api.ts that goes through api/client.ts.
  end note
}

cloud "FastAPI backend\n(see DATA_FLOW_DIAGRAM.puml for what\nhappens the instant a request crosses)" as backend #FDF0E3

' -------- dependency arrows (who calls / imports whom) --------
entry --> routes
routes --> shellpkg
routes --> authpkg
routes --> verwaltung
shellpkg --> hookspkg
shellpkg --> protopkg
authpkg --> client
protopkg --> client
verwaltung --> client
client --> backend
protopkg --> i18npkg
verwaltung --> i18npkg
shellpkg --> themepkg

legend right
  |= Colour |= Meaning |
  | <back:#FFF3CD>   </back> | App wiring / composition root - read these FIRST |
  | <back:#E8F4FD>   </back> | Cross-cutting concerns, used by nearly everything |
  | <back:#FDEBD0>   </back> | Feature areas: the protocol form, and administration |
  | <back:#D4EDDA>   </back> | Reusable building blocks / pure logic (heavily tested) |
  | <back:#FDF0E3>   </back> | Outside the frontend entirely |
endlegend

@enduml

@startuml FRONTEND_ARCHITECTURE_DIAGRAM_PROTOKOLL
' ==============================================================================
' DIAGRAM 2: inside protokoll/, the survey form itself.
'
' This is where nearly all the code is. Diagram 1 above shows this whole package
' as one box; everything here sits inside it. The one thing drawn from outside is
' api/client.ts, because every folder here that talks to the server goes through
' it, and that is the point worth seeing.
' ==============================================================================

skinparam componentStyle rectangle
skinparam packageStyle folder
skinparam wrapWidth 260
skinparam maxMessageSize 260

header FRONTEND FOLDER MAP - frontend/src/protokoll/**
title
  DIAGRAM 2 of 2 - INSIDE protokoll/
  the survey form: its screens, its fields, its rules, its two PDF directions
end title

package "protokoll/  (the feature: the survey form itself)" as protopkg #FDEBD0 {

  package "liste/  (Meine Protokolle)" as liste {
    [ProtokolleSeite.tsx]
    [ProtokollTabelle.tsx, ProtokollZeile.tsx]
    [LoeschenDialog.tsx, Loeschfehler.tsx, useLoeschen.ts]
    [LeererZustand.tsx, Ladefehler.tsx, anzeige.ts]
    note bottom of liste
      "*Seite.tsx" = **page / route container components**. Each one
      is what a route in routes.tsx points at. Pattern: a Seite
      component's job is to fetch data (via useQuery) and hand it down
      as props to smaller, "dumber" presentational components below -
      it rarely renders much markup itself. This is the classic
      "container vs. presentational component" split.
    end note
  }

  package "pruefliste/  (the review queue, feature 12)" as pruefliste {
    [PrueflisteSeite.tsx]
    [PrueflistenTabelle.tsx, PrueflistenZeile.tsx]
    [Filterleiste.tsx, Pager.tsx]
    [parameter.ts, pfad.ts, abfragen.ts, api.ts]
    [KeineBerechtigung.tsx, LeererZustand.tsx, Ladefehler.tsx]
    note bottom of pruefliste
      The one page that reads across every account. Its filters live
      in the address bar rather than in component state (parameter.ts
      is the pure translation between the two), which is what lets a
      filtered queue be shared as a link, and what feature 12d needed
      to walk the list from a single protocol's own URL.
    end note
  }

  package "pruefung/  (the reviewer's screen, feature 11)" as pruefung {
    [PruefungsSeite.tsx]
    [Entscheidungspanel.tsx, useUebergang.ts]
    [Kopfzeile.tsx, Pruefungsrail.tsx, Panel.tsx]
    [Uebersichtsleiste.tsx, Listennavigation.tsx]
    [Verlauf.tsx, verlaufsbeschriftung.ts]
    [AenderungAngefordert.tsx, aenderungsbitte.ts]
    [entscheidungen.ts, entscheidungsfehler.ts]
    [Aufnahmeblock.tsx, Uebergangsfehler.tsx]
    note bottom of pruefung
      Accept, reject, request changes, and the history of what was
      decided. The state machine itself is the backend's; this folder
      holds which transitions a given account may ASK for
      (entscheidungen.ts), what a rejection or change request must
      carry (aenderungsbitte.ts), and how a refused transition is put
      into words (entscheidungsfehler.ts) - all pure functions, tested
      without a browser.
    end note
  }

  package "ProtokollSeite.tsx + ProtokollFormular.tsx  (the form itself)" as formcore {
    [ProtokollSeite.tsx] as PSeite
    [ProtokollFormular.tsx] as PFormular
    [useProtokollZustand.tsx] as PZustand
    [abschnitte.ts (the 7 sections + paths)]
    [AbschnittNav.tsx, AbschnittMenu.tsx, AbschnittWechsel.tsx]
    [ProtokollKopf.tsx, ProtokollTitel.tsx, Statusabzeichen.tsx]
    [SpeicherAnzeige.tsx, SpeicherProblem.tsx]
    [VerwerfenDialog.tsx, optionen.ts]
    [ProtokollLadefehler.tsx, ProtokollNichtGefunden.tsx]
    note bottom of formcore
      ProtokollSeite.tsx (a page container) loads the Entwurf via
      useQuery, then hands it to ProtokollFormular.tsx, which sets up
      ONE React Hook Form instance for the WHOLE ~338-field document
      (not one form per section) and shares it via <FormProvider> -
      React's Context API used specifically to avoid "prop drilling"
      (passing `form` down through 5 layers of components by hand).
      Any descendant can call useFormContext() to reach it directly.

      useProtokollZustand.tsx is the shared front half of that: the
      four dead ends both id-addressed pages hit before they reach a
      protocol (loading, load failure, not found, no permission). It
      hands back a ReactNode rather than a union to switch on, because
      every one of those states has exactly one right rendering and
      both callers want it.

      SpeicherAnzeige is the short save indicator; SpeicherProblem is
      the panel that appears only when a save failed and has room to
      say what to do. Both read the one SaveState from useAutoSave.
    end note
  }

  package "abschnitte/  (the 7 sections' markup)" as abschnitte {
    [AbschnittInhalt.tsx, koerper.ts]
    [Abschnitt1.tsx .. Abschnitt7.tsx]
    [teil1/ Anlass, Bearbeiter, Probestrecke]
    [teil2/ Messdaten, Hydrologie]
    [teil3/ Umland, Ufer, Gewaessersohle (the % blocks)]
    [teil4/ Strukturen, Einfluesse, Bewirtschaftung, Bemerkungen]
    [teil5/ Ausruestung, Befischte Bereiche]
    [teil6/ the catch table (ArtenTabelle + cells)]
    [teil7/ Kartenausschnitt, Fotos (attachments)]
    note bottom of abschnitte
      Mostly composition: these components lay out FeldText/FeldAuswahl/
      etc. (see felder/ below) into the actual form sections. Logic
      lives in regeln/ and in the per-part *.ts files beside the
      markup (gruppen.ts, bloecke.ts, tabelle.ts, zeilen.ts), kept OUT
      of the JSX so it is unit-testable without rendering anything.

      Seven sections, not the paper form's six: attachments are
      section 7, because they are a separate thing to do and the step
      bar is how a surveyor finds anything in a protocol this long.
      The legacy PDF decides which fields exist and what the rules
      are, never how the screen is laid out.
    end note
  }

  package "felder/  (reusable, presentational field components)" as felder #D4EDDA {
    [FeldRahmen.tsx] as Rahmen
    [FeldText.tsx, FeldAuswahl.tsx, FeldRadio.tsx]
    [FeldProzent.tsx, FeldDatum.tsx, FeldHaken.tsx]
    [FeldSuche.tsx, Optionssuche.tsx, Suche.tsx]
    [fehler.ts (useFeldFehler hook)]
    [rahmen.ts (feldAria, fehlerId helpers)]
    [pflicht.ts (which answers are required)]
    note bottom of felder
      **The reusable UI component library for this app**, built on MUI.
      Every one of the ~338 fields is one of these ~8 components, each
      wired to React Hook Form via register(name) (an UNCONTROLLED
      input - see DATA_FLOW_DIAGRAM.puml step 1). FeldRahmen.tsx is
      shared "chrome" (label + error text + accessibility attributes)
      that every Feld* wraps itself in, so keyboard/label/ARIA
      behaviour is written once and inherited everywhere - exactly the
      "theme/build a component once" rule from coding-standards.md.

      pflicht.ts reads the required-field list from the SAME file the
      backend reads (database/seed/form_version_20260609/
      pflichtfelder.json, aliased in from outside frontend/). Until
      feature 11c the two sides kept their own lists and drifted, and
      the drift was invisible: an asterisk nothing actually checks
      looks exactly like one that works.
    end note
  }

  package "entwurf/  (draft persistence: the autosave engine)" as entwurf #D4EDDA {
    [useAutoSave.ts] as useAutoSave
    [api.ts (legeEntwurfAn, speichereAntworten,\nleseProtokollEin)] as entwurfApi
    [sicherung.ts (localStorage safety copy)] as sicherung
    [browserSpeicher.ts (storage that may throw)]
    [SicherungAngebot.tsx (offer to restore it)]
    [bereitstellen.ts (make the record exist)]
    [speicherzustand.ts, abfragen.ts]
    [neu.ts, titel.ts, typen.ts]
    note bottom of entwurf
      The engine traced end-to-end in DATA_FLOW_DIAGRAM.puml.
      useAutoSave.ts is a **custom hook** - the standard React way to
      package up stateful logic (a debounce timer, a form.watch()
      subscription, retry/conflict bookkeeping) so a component can use
      it with one line, without that logic cluttering the component's
      own body. Deliberately calls apiAnfrage() directly instead of
      useMutation() - a documented, reasoned EXCEPTION to the "use
      TanStack Query for saves" rule, because useMutation's own
      re-renders were measurably too slow for a ~338-field form.

      browserSpeicher.ts exists because reaching for window.localStorage
      is not safe on its own: a private window or blocked site data
      makes the property access itself throw, before any key is read.
      bereitstellen.ts is the same "the first thing put in creates the
      record" moment as the first keystroke, for section 7, which has
      no fields to type in - an attachment is addressed through its
      protocol, so the protocol has to exist first.
    end note
  }

  package "regeln/  (validation rules, mirrors the backend)" as regeln #D4EDDA {
    [schema.ts (the combined Zod schema)]
    [regel.ts (the shared rule shape)]
    [prozent.ts, hydrologie.ts, vorfluter.ts]
    [koordinaten.ts, arten.ts, ausruestung.ts]
    [einfluesse.ts, monitoring.ts, schaetzwert.ts]
    [useHydrologieAbgleich.ts, useNachpruefung.ts]
    note bottom of regeln
      **Plain, framework-free functions** (no React, no fetch) - the
      same "logic as pure functions" rule coding-standards.md applies
      to the backend's own formregeln/. Each file here is the
      CLIENT-SIDE half of a rule the backend also enforces in
      Pydantic/Python (e.g. "percentages must sum to 100"), and the
      two are meant to be read and changed together. Zod = instant
      feedback only; the backend is the authoritative gate. Being
      plain functions is what makes every *.test.ts beside them
      possible without spinning up a browser.
    end note
  }

  package "absenden/  (handing the protocol in)" as absenden {
    [useAbsenden.ts]
    [AbsendenBlock.tsx, AbsendeErgebnis.tsx]
    [AbsendeProbleme.tsx, gruppierung.ts]
    [gemerkt.ts (problems that survive a reload)]
    [verortung.ts, useErledigte.ts]
    note bottom of absenden
      A submit sends NO answers of its own: whatever is stored is what
      gets handed in, so it first waits for the automatic save to come
      to rest (useAutoSave's bereitZumAbsenden).

      A refusal lists the fields that stopped it. gemerkt.ts keeps
      that list in this browser so it survives navigating between
      sections, gruppierung.ts folds it by section and verortung.ts
      says which section a given field path lives in - so a problem is
      shown next to the thing it concerns, with a compact count
      elsewhere, rather than as one long list at the top of the page.
    end note
  }

  package "einlesen/  (reading the legacy PDF in, feature 23a-d)" as einlesen {
    [useEinlesen.ts]
    [useEinlesebericht.ts, bericht.ts]
    [EinleseBanner.tsx, EinleseFehler.tsx]
    [FehlendeBilder.tsx]
    note bottom of einlesen
      Upload a protocol filled in with the old Acrobat form and open
      it as a DRAFT with the answers already in place. Never as a
      submission: the legacy form has known validation bugs, so an
      imported protocol arrives with its problems listed and is
      checked by exactly the same rules as anything typed in.

      An import is a way to keep filling in the Acrobat form and still
      file through the app. It is not a migration tool for records
      already in FiaKa.

      Three different things can be wrong, and the person does
      something different about each. Rule violations reuse
      absenden/gemerkt.ts, because they are the same shape a refused
      submit produces. The other two - an answer that could not be
      read, and a photograph the file carries that is not in the
      protocol - have no such home, and bericht.ts is it.
    end note
  }

  package "ausgabe/  (handing a protocol back as a PDF, 23e)" as ausgabe {
    [useHerunterladen.ts]
    [HerunterladenKnopf.tsx]
    [dateiname.ts]
    note bottom of ausgabe
      The backend builds the document and serves it as a file with a
      proper name, so every copy looks the same wherever it was saved
      from. It is **a document of our own, not a copy of the form**:
      a plain readable file the surveyor saves like a print-out. Reached from both the form's header and the
      reviewer's screen. Goes through apiDatei() rather than a plain
      <a href>, so a refused download says why in German beside the
      button instead of showing a raw JSON error page.
    end note
  }

  package "anlagen/  (attachments, feature 3d)" as anlagen {
    [useAnlagen.ts]
    [api.ts, abfragen.ts, typen.ts]
    [regeln.ts (size, type, count)]
    [altbestand.ts]
    note bottom of anlagen
      The blocks in section 7 (abschnitte/teil7/) never apply the
      rules themselves and never talk to the API; useAnlagen.ts is
      what they call. Attachments count as part of the protocol, so
      anything that reports on a protocol has to account for them and
      not only for the typed answers.
    end note
  }

  package "nurlesen/  (read-only rendering mode)" as nurlesen {
    [kontext.ts]
    [Feldwert.tsx, wert.ts]
    [ProtokollNurLesen.tsx, ProtokollAnsicht.tsx]
    [ArtenNurLesen.tsx, artenzeilen.ts]
    [AnlagenNurLesen.tsx]
    note bottom of nurlesen
      kontext.ts is a small React Context ("am I in read-only mode?").
      Every Feld* component checks useNurLesen() and, if true, renders
      a plain <Feldwert> instead of an editable input (see FeldText.tsx's
      own `if (nurLesen) {...}` branch). One flag, read from Context,
      instead of passing a `readOnly` prop through every layer by hand -
      the same Context-avoids-prop-drilling pattern FormProvider uses.

      This is what lets the reviewer's screen show the whole protocol
      without a second set of components that could drift from the
      editable ones.
    end note
  }
}

package "api/  (Diagram 1)" as apipkg #D4EDDA {
  [client.ts (apiAnfrage, apiDatei)] as client
}

